# Decision 0010: Kotlin artifact model and the `kotlin-native` driver pair

Record numbers 0007 and 0008 are taken by accepted sibling records that have not
yet landed on `main`; 0009 is claimed concurrently by `TASK-260728-12pnm1`
(Rust) and `TASK-260728-1jafds` (hardened execution profile). This record takes
0010 so that the set can land in any order. If a lower free number is available
when this record lands, it renumbers rather than contests, exactly as
`TASK-260728-2spy93` did when 0007 was claimed.

## Context

Decision 0008 reserved six additional-language driver identifiers, two of them
Kotlin — `kotlin-native-v1` and `kotlin-native-repository-v1` — and made
`TASK-260728-168smo` responsible for one thing the other two language contracts
did not have to answer:

> `TASK-260728-168smo` additionally decides the Kotlin backend **within**, not
> around, section 3; if no Kotlin backend satisfies it, both Kotlin identifiers
> are retired unused.

Decision 0008 section 3 admits exactly one artifact class,
`native-executable-v1`: one bounded regular file, named solely by the manager,
directly executable by the host program loader using only base-installation
libraries, never executed by the manager. It rejects `runtime-bundle` — "any
artifact that requires a manager-generated launcher, an interpreter or virtual
machine, a classpath or module path, a runtime image, or more than one published
file" — for four independent reasons, and forbids readmitting it by widening
`native-executable-v1`.

Decision 0007 reserved the `kotlin` toolchain entry and the companion-only `jdk`
identifier, and required this decision to supply every registry field "on a
qualified host", plus `kotlin`'s single metadata file and field.

`TASK-260729-rhjxtx` then measured the host situation and recorded an honest
absence: **no Kotlin/Native distribution exists on either reachable host**, the
Kotlin compiler that is present is the wrong backend, and Kotlin has no
project-metadata file readable without executing package code. It closed with
the consequence for this task — mint a driver-defined metadata file, or accept
that decision 0008 section 10's retirement clause applies.

This decision selects the artifact model, fixes everything about the pair that
is decidable without a Kotlin/Native host, states exactly what remains
host-dependent, and fixes the retirement trigger now rather than leaving the
reservation to linger. It defines no schema file, regenerates no vector, creates
no release metadata, advances no pin, and **makes no platform claim**.

Both identifiers remain reserved. Nothing here admits them to the wire.

## Evidence status

This record separates three kinds of statement and never blurs them.

- **DECIDED** — host-independent, settled by this record.
- **MEASURED** — measured on this host, with the command and exit code recorded
  in `TASK-260728-168smo_command-evidence.log`. Every measurement in this record
  was taken against the **Kotlin/JVM** CLI distribution (`kotlinc-jvm 2.4.10`)
  and OpenJDK 26.0.1, because that is what exists. Where a measurement is used
  to reason about Kotlin/Native, the inference is labelled and is an obligation,
  not a claim.
- **UNVERIFIED** — a named question that only a Kotlin/Native host can answer,
  carried into the qualification protocol of section 9 with a decision rule
  attached, so the answer cannot be supplied by assertion later.

The host inventory of `TASK-260729-rhjxtx` was re-confirmed for this record:
`kotlinc-native`, `konanc`, and `~/.konan` are absent; `kotlinc` resolves to a
JVM-backend distribution whose `bin/` holds `kotlinc-jvm`, `kotlinc-js`,
`kotlinc-wasm`, `kapt`, and `kotlinr` — and **no** native front end. Nothing was
installed, downloaded, updated, activated, or switched.

## Decision

### 1. The artifact model: Kotlin/Native, and nothing else in this version

**DECIDED.** The Kotlin family is admitted through the **Kotlin/Native** backend
producing a single native executable, or not at all. Both Kotlin/JVM artifact
shapes are rejected for this version, and the rejection is a consequence of
decision 0008 section 3 rather than a preference:

| Candidate | Published files | Directly loadable | Verdict |
|---|---|---|---|
| Kotlin/JVM thin JAR plus operator-installed JRE | 1 | no — needs a JVM and a classpath | rejected: `runtime-bundle` |
| Kotlin/JVM fat JAR (`-include-runtime`) | 1 | no — still needs a JVM | rejected: `runtime-bundle` |
| Kotlin/JVM plus `jlink`/`jpackage` runtime image | many | launcher plus runtime tree | rejected: `runtime-bundle` |
| Kotlin/JVM plus GraalVM `native-image` | 1 | yes | rejected: see section 12 |
| **Kotlin/Native `-produce program`** | **1** | **yes** | **selected, subject to section 9** |

The fat JAR deserves the explicit row because it is the shape most often
described as self-contained. It is one file, and it still fails section 3's
third bullet: a JAR is not directly executable by the host program loader, and
making it so requires either a manager-generated launcher or a `runtime-bundle`
class. One file is necessary and not sufficient.

`jdk` therefore stays a **companion** identifier under this decision and is
never the artifact's runtime. Decision 0007 admitted it "if
`TASK-260728-168smo` selects a JVM artifact model"; this record selects a native
artifact model and nonetheless requires `jdk`, for a different reason fixed in
section 5 — the Kotlin/Native *compiler* is JVM-hosted, so the JDK is a build
input, not an execution-time dependency of the produced artifact. That
distinction is what keeps the selection inside section 3: the published file
depends on no runtime the manager would have to install, verify, or fingerprint
at install time.

A future Kotlin/JVM driver, if a `runtime-bundle` profile is ever reviewed and
accepted, MUST use a different family segment and MUST NOT reuse either
identifier reserved here, exactly as decision 0008 section 2 requires.

### 2. Identifiers, and no new names

**DECIDED.** This record coins no identifier. It adopts, unchanged, the two
names decision 0008 section 2 reserved:

| Driver | Source mode | Receipt schema | Execution policy | Status |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `kotlin-native-v1` | local snapshot | 3 | `manager-worker-v2` | reserved |
| `kotlin-native-repository-v1` | external repository | 4 | `manager-worker-v2` | reserved |

Reservation is not admission. Until `TASK-260728-251p01` moves them in the same
change that mints the schema admitting them, every schema MUST reject both and a
manager MUST treat both as unknown drivers. Section 10 fixes the conditions
under which they are instead retired unused.

### 3. The project-metadata file: `kotlin-native-module.json`

**DECIDED.** Decision 0008 section 4 requires each local driver to bind exactly
one closed **driver-defined** project-metadata file that exists directly in the
build root and is the nearest ancestor of `source_dir`. Decision 0007 section
1.3 requires the `kotlin` entry to name "exactly one file and field".

Kotlin has no such file. The candidates and why each fails:

| Candidate | Why rejected |
|---|---|
| `build.gradle.kts` / `build.gradle` | Reading it is executing it. `TASK-260729-rhjxtx` **measured** that the pure metadata query `gradle properties` compiles the build script as a program source unit (`_BuildScript_`) before it can answer. This is the generic Gradle escape hatch the epic exists to refuse. |
| `settings.gradle{,.kts}` | Same class, same measurement. |
| `gradle.properties` | Not executable, but it is a Gradle input whose semantics Curator would be reinterpreting, and whose presence asserts a build model this driver does not run. Its `kotlin.*` keys are compiler and daemon settings — package-selected build behaviour under a different name. |
| `pom.xml` | Parsable without execution, but the Maven project model is defined by its `<build><plugins>` graph; reading one field while ignoring that graph would let a package ship a plugin declaration Curator silently does not honour, and would license authors to believe Maven runs. |
| A bare marker file with no field | Fails decision 0007 section 1.3, which requires one file **and one field**. |
| `.kotlin-version` plain text | No schema version, no closure, no way to reject an added line; invites the free-text ecosystem grammar problem decision 0007 section 2 deliberately keeps off Curator-owned surfaces. |

The driver therefore binds a Curator-owned file, `kotlin-native-module.json`,
which is exactly what "driver-defined" in decision 0008 section 4 permits and
what `skill-build.json` already establishes as protocol style for a
Curator-owned file inside package-supplied source:

```json
{"schema_version": 1, "kotlin_version": "2.4.0"}
```

It contains exactly `schema_version` and `kotlin_version`, both REQUIRED, with
`additionalProperties: false`. `schema_version` is the `const` integer `1` and
participates in the file-shape gate only. `kotlin_version` is the sole
`metadata_sources` field of the `kotlin` entry: a canonical
`major.minor.patch` triple in decision 0007 section 2.1's grammar, asserting the
Kotlin compiler version the sources are written against.

Three properties follow, and each is a simplification rather than a special
case:

1. **The classifier is two classes, not seven.** Because Curator owns the
   grammar, `kotlin_version` is either a well-formed canonical triple —
   disposition `compared`, rule "above resolved → mismatch" — or it is not, in
   which case it is `build_toolchain_metadata_mismatch` at the file-shape gate.
   There is no document layer, no ecosystem grammar layer, and no edition floor
   to be independent of, because there is no ecosystem grammar in play.
2. **The security partition `F` is empty.** The value space is a version and
   nothing else; no spelling of it names where a toolchain comes from. Decision
   0007's P1 and P2 therefore collapse to the satisfiable equality
   `C = Upstream`, where `Upstream` is Curator's own canonical grammar, so
   alignment is exact by construction rather than by tracking an upstream
   artifact that moves independently.
3. **It is inert to the compiler.** The file selects nothing, is never passed to
   the compiler, and contributes no argument. Its two jobs are to mark the build
   root boundary and to carry one assertion Stage B compares. An author who
   deletes it gets a deterministic Curator rejection and an unchanged compiler.

The honest cost, stated rather than buried: a Kotlin build root is **not** an
IDE-openable Kotlin project, because section 6's allow-list excludes every file
a Kotlin IDE project needs. Section 11 states that consequence in full.

`source_dir` maps to exactly one compiled program without discovery, without a
new command member, and without naming an entry point: the program is the
recursive `.kt` source set under `source_dir` compiled in `program` mode with
the compiler's default entry point. Zero or multiple entry points is a compiler
error and therefore deterministic; the manager never names, searches for, or
infers one.

### 4. The command surfaces

**DECIDED.** Nothing Kotlin-specific is added to either command shape. The local
command is decision 0008's `buildCommandV8` with `driver` the `const`
`kotlin-native-v1`:

```json
{"type":"build","driver":"kotlin-native-v1","source_dir":"build/cmd/tool",
 "toolchain":{"id":"kotlin","version":{"kind":"at_least","min":"2.1.0"}}}
```

The external command is `repositoryBuildCommandV2` with `driver` the `const`
`kotlin-native-repository-v1`, and the descriptor target is
`skillBuildTargetV2` with the same `const`, `build_root`, `source_dir`, and the
OPTIONAL `toolchain`. `toolchain.id` MUST be `kotlin`, the registry primary; the
companion `jdk` is not expressible in a manifest or a descriptor, because it is
pipeline structure rather than a property of the source.

No member is added anywhere. In particular, no manifest, descriptor, or metadata
file may express a target, a Kotlin/Native target triple, an entry point, a
module name, a klib, a library, a linker option, an opt-in marker, a language or
API version flag, a compiler plugin, a plugin option, a memory model, a GC
selection, a bitcode or debug setting, a cache mode, a JVM option, a classpath,
a `KONAN_DATA_DIR`, or a dependency source.

### 5. The trusted toolchain closure and the process graph

This is the hardest question in the Kotlin contract, and it is answered by
measurement on the launcher shape plus one named obligation.

**MEASURED — the shipped Kotlin CLI launcher is inadmissible.** With `PATH` set
to a directory of logging shims that record their name and argv and exit 127,
running the distribution's own `bin/kotlinc` resolved from `PATH`, in order:

| Resolution | Origin |
|---|---|
| `bash` | the script's `#!/usr/bin/env bash` shebang |
| `uname` | `cygwin` detection |
| `dirname` | `findKotlinHome` |
| `java -version` | `findJavaVersion` |
| `java <full compiler command line>` | the compiler itself |

Four `PATH` resolutions plus the shebang, and the compiler process is `java`
resolved **by name**. The script also honours `JAVACMD`, `JAVA_HOME`,
`JAVA_OPTS`, `KOTLIN_RUNNER`, `KOTLIN_COMPILER`, and `KOTLIN_TOOL` from the
inherited environment — `KOTLIN_COMPILER` selects the compiler main class and
`KOTLIN_TOOL` appends to the compiler classpath. That is a wrapper resolving
executables outside its own tree and taking environment-selected pipeline
inputs: precisely what decision 0007 section 3 forbids of a primary executable
and what decision 0008 section 6 item 3 forbids of the process graph.

**MEASURED — bypassing the launcher closes all of it.** Invoking the JDK's own
`bin/java` directly, with `-cp` naming jars inside the Kotlin distribution and
the compiler main class as an argument, under the identical shim `PATH`:

| Run | `PATH` resolutions | Exit | Artifact |
|---|---|---|---|
| control: shipped `bin/kotlinc` launcher | **4** (plus shebang) | 127 | none |
| direct `<jdk_root>/bin/java -cp <kotlin_root>/…` | **0** | 0 | produced |

`<jdk_root>/bin/java` is a `Mach-O 64-bit executable arm64` — a regular
executable at a fixed relpath inside the tree being fingerprinted, satisfying
decision 0007 section 3 exactly. Because the launcher script is never run, none
of `JAVACMD`, `JAVA_HOME`, `JAVA_OPTS`, `KOTLIN_RUNNER`, `KOTLIN_COMPILER`, or
`KOTLIN_TOOL` can reach the pipeline at all; they are closed structurally rather
than by neutralisation.

**DECIDED — the process graph.** `manager-worker-v2`'s two lower nodes bind, for
both Kotlin drivers, to:

```text
manager parent
  -> identity-verified manager-owned worker
       -> <jdk_root>/bin/java                      (the trusted launcher)
            -> the Kotlin/Native compiler, in-process in that JVM,
               loaded by -cp from <kotlin_root>
                 -> fingerprinted regular executables inside <kotlin_root>
                    (the code generator, assembler, archiver and linker)
```

The driver MUST NOT invoke `bin/kotlinc-native`, `bin/konanc`, `bin/kotlinc`,
`bin/run_konan`, or any other launcher script shipped by either distribution, on
any platform, including the version probe. `toolchain_identities` is the ordered
two-element array `[curator-kotlin-toolchain-v1, curator-jdk-toolchain-v1]`, the
primary first. Both roots are fully fingerprinted; neither is data-only, because
`<kotlin_root>` contributes the compiler jars and — subject to the obligation
below — the native tools the compiler spawns.

**UNVERIFIED, and carried into section 9 as obligations K-1 through K-4.** Four
things about Kotlin/Native cannot be settled here:

- **K-1, launcher shape.** That the Kotlin/Native distribution exposes its
  compiler as a JVM main class reachable by `-cp`, and the exact jar relpath and
  main class. The measurement above is on the JVM-backend distribution; the two
  distributions are different artifacts and this host has only one of them. If
  the Kotlin/Native distribution instead ships a self-contained regular
  executable that resolves nothing outside its own tree, that executable becomes
  the trusted launcher, `jdk` drops out of the companion list, and the design is
  strictly simpler; both shapes are admissible, and no third shape is.
- **K-2, child processes.** That every executable the compiler spawns — LLVM
  code generator, assembler, archiver, linker — is a regular file inside
  `<kotlin_root>`. Kotlin/Native has historically resolved Apple platform
  linking through Xcode. If a qualification run resolves any executable that
  cannot be bound to `kotlin` or `jdk`, that platform is **not admitted**:
  decision 0007's closed identifier set is `{go, rust, swift, kotlin, jdk}`,
  there is no identifier for a platform SDK that contributes a *process*, and
  Rust's data-only SDK root is not available as a precedent for an executed
  linker.
- **K-3, self-containment.** That the distribution performs no dependency
  download. Kotlin/Native has historically materialised LLVM and sysroots into
  `~/.konan` on first use. Section 7 fixes the mitigation and section 9 fixes
  the test.
- **K-4, output naming.** The exact filename the compiler produces for a given
  `-o` value, including any `.kexe` or `.exe` suffix the compiler appends.

None of the four is answered by assertion in this record.

**DECIDED — a narrow reading of decision 0007 section 3, not a widening.** Under
shape K-1(a) the `kotlin` entry's fingerprinted root contributes no launcher
process, and the primary executable of the *pipeline* is the companion's
`bin/java`. This is a completion of a reserved entry within the mandate decision
0007 section 1.3 grants, not a widening of its section 3: every executed binary
is still a regular file at a fixed relpath inside a fingerprinted,
operator-trusted root, nothing is package-selected, no wrapper is run, and no
auto-install is introduced. It is strictly stronger than honouring the shipped
launcher would be. If decision 0007's owner rejects the reading, the consequence
is section 10's retirement, not a compensating wrapper.

### 6. The pre-compile rejection matrix: an allow-list, not a deny-list

**DECIDED.** Decision 0008 section 7 requires an exhaustive, deterministic,
pre-compile rejection matrix over every package-selected code-execution surface.
Kotlin's surfaces are unusually broad — a general-purpose script engine
(Gradle), a script dialect the compiler itself can execute (`.kts`), annotation
processing (kapt, KSP), compiler plugins, and C interop — so an enumerated
deny-list would be an open set and could not be exhaustive.

The matrix is therefore a **closed allow-list over the compiler-visible tree**,
on the same principle decision 0008 section 11 item 6 applies to schema member
sets. Computed from the validated immutable snapshot, before the compile phase,
by a manager-side walk that runs no compiler:

The build root and every directory below it admit exactly:

1. directories, whose names match `^[A-Za-z0-9_][A-Za-z0-9_.-]*$`;
2. regular files whose names match `^[A-Za-z0-9_][A-Za-z0-9_.-]*\.kt$`; and
3. exactly one additional regular file, `kotlin-native-module.json`, directly in
   the build root and nowhere else.

Every other entry is rejected under `build_package_code_execution_forbidden`
with a per-surface diagnostic. There is no exception, no opt-out, no advisory
mode, and no configuration that widens it.

This single rule subsumes, by construction rather than by enumeration:

| Surface | How it is excluded |
|---|---|
| Gradle build scripts, settings, wrapper, `gradle.properties`, `.gradle/` | not `.kt`, not the metadata file |
| Maven `pom.xml`, `.mvn/`, `mvnw` | same |
| Kotlin script sources `.kts` | not `.kt` |
| kapt/KSP configuration, `META-INF/services`, ProGuard rules | same |
| C-interop `.def`, headers, `.c`/`.m`/`.cpp`, objects, archives, shared libraries | same |
| prebuilt `.klib`, `.jar`, `.aar` | same |
| symlinks, devices, sockets, FIFOs, hard-linked escapes | not regular files or directories |
| dotfiles of every kind | leading character class |
| **any name beginning with `@`** | leading character class |

The `@` row is the one that is not obvious, and it is the reason the leading
character class is load-bearing rather than cosmetic.

**MEASURED — a build-root filename is an argv-injection channel.** The Kotlin
CLI expands a bare `@`-prefixed argument as a response file. With a file named
`@inject` containing `-script`, and a `.kts` file in the same directory:

| Argument form | Expanded | Outcome |
|---|---|---|
| `@inject` (bare relative) | yes | `-script` injected; **package code executed**; marker file written; exit **0** |
| `/abs/path/@inject` | no | `error: source entry is not a Kotlin file`, exit 1 |
| `./@inject` | no | same error, exit 1 |
| `@nonexistent` | n/a | `warning: argfile not found`, exit **0** — a missing response file is not an error |

So a package that ships a file named `@x` in a directory the driver enumerates
can execute arbitrary Kotlin at build time, through the compiler alone, with no
Gradle anywhere and with a zero exit code. Two independent layers close it, and
the contract requires both:

- **the normative layer** is the allow-list above, which rejects the name before
  the compile phase, as decision 0008 section 7 demands; and
- **the structural backstop** is section 7's argv discipline — every
  package-derived path is passed in absolute form — which the table above
  measures as sufficient on its own. It is a backstop and not the rule, because
  relying on argv shaping alone would put the whole surface one implementation
  slip away from execution.

A `.kts` file that reaches the compiler *without* `-script` was measured to be
compiled into a class and **not** executed (no marker; a `.class` produced, exit
0) on the JVM backend. That is why `.kts` is rejected as a build-model surface
rather than described as immediate execution: the honest statement is that it is
inert alone and is the payload half of the `@` vector. Its behaviour under the
native backend is UNVERIFIED and is obligation K-5.

The four argv-only plugin surfaces — `-Xplugin=<path>`, `-Xcompiler-plugin=…`,
`-P plugin:…`, and `-script-templates` — are **structurally unreachable**,
because the closed command surface of section 4 gives a package no way to supply
an argument and the driver's vector is fixed. They are named here so that the
matrix is exhaustive over surfaces rather than over file names, and so that any
future change that lets package data reach argv is visibly a change to this row.

### 7. The worker session, the argument vector, and the environment

**DECIDED — session shape.** A `kotlin-native-v1` or
`kotlin-native-repository-v1` operation uses **zero** graph-phase commands and
exactly one compile-phase command. Decision 0008 section 7's "at most one" graph
phase is what admits this, and Kotlin is the case it was written for: there is
no dependency resolution to perform, because section 6 admits no package-supplied
library and section 4 admits no dependency declaration, so the source set is
decided by a manager-side directory walk rather than by asking a tool.

**DECIDED — argument vector shape.** One process, one command, no retry, no
daemon, no second phase:

```text
<jdk_root>/bin/java
  -Dfile.encoding=UTF-8 -Duser.language=en -Duser.country=US
  -cp <kotlin_root>/<compiler-jar-relpath>        [K-1]
  <compiler-main-class>                            [K-1]
  -produce program
  -target <resolved native target>                 [K-6]
  -o <operation-private-staging>/<command>
  <absolute path to source 1>
  …
  <absolute path to source N>
```

with these binding rules:

- sources are the recursive `.kt` set under `source_dir`, enumerated by the
  manager, sorted by Unicode-scalar order of relative path, and passed as
  **absolute** paths — never as a directory source root, because a directory
  hands file discovery to the compiler and would put files outside the walked
  allow-list into the compilation;
- no entry point, module name, opt-in, language-version, API-version, optimisation,
  debug, cache, memory-model, or plugin argument is passed, and none is
  package-derivable;
- `-target` names the host's own default native target only. **Cross-compilation
  is not admitted in this version.** A package cannot request a target, the
  driver never selects one, and the "representable but unserved target" hazard
  that Rust and Swift both had to gate against therefore does not arise here;
- the exact spelling of `-produce`/`-target`/`-o` and the produced filename are
  obligations K-4 and K-6.

**DECIDED — operation-private environment.** In addition to the
`manager-worker-v2` portable control set, the environment MUST:

| Variable | Action | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `JDK_JAVA_OPTIONS` | unset | **MEASURED**: honoured by the JVM launcher; injects arbitrary JVM options |
| `JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS` | unset | **MEASURED**: honoured; same |
| `_JAVA_OPTIONS` | unset | same family |
| `CLASSPATH` | unset | would extend the compiler classpath |
| `JAVA_HOME`, `JAVACMD`, `JAVA_OPTS` | unset | launcher-script inputs; closed already by bypassing the launcher, unset as defence in depth |
| `KOTLIN_HOME`, `KOTLIN_COMPILER`, `KOTLIN_TOOL`, `KOTLIN_RUNNER` | unset | same |
| `KONAN_DATA_DIR` | **set** to an operation-private empty directory | prevents any write to or read from `~/.konan` and makes obligation K-3 decidable — a distribution that needs to fetch fails visibly instead of silently populating a shared cache |

Both `JDK_JAVA_OPTIONS` and `JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS` write a "Picked up …" notice to
**stderr**, and `java --version` stdout stayed byte-stable at 148 bytes under
both. That is why section 8's probe reads stdout: the version surface does not
move even when the environment has been tampered with, so the probe fails closed
on the environment check rather than mis-parsing.

### 8. Registry entries

**DECIDED — shape.** The `kotlin` entry is primary for both drivers with the
ordered companion list `[jdk]` under shape K-1(a), or the empty companion list
under shape K-1(b). The `jdk` entry is companion-only, has no
`metadata_sources`, and is not expressible in package data.

**Fields supplied now:**

| Field | `kotlin` | `jdk` |
|---|---|---|
| `toolchain_id` | `kotlin` | `jdk` |
| `fingerprint_algorithm` | `curator-kotlin-toolchain-v1` | `curator-jdk-toolchain-v1` |
| `metadata_sources` | `kotlin-native-module.json` `kotlin_version` | none |
| `primary_relpath` | **obligation K-1** | `bin/java`, `bin/java.exe` on Windows |
| `probe` | **obligation K-7** | `java --version`, from a manager-owned empty working directory under the operation-private environment |
| `normalization` | **obligation K-7** | line 1 of **stdout**; `^\S+ (\d+)(?:\.(\d+))?(?:\.(\d+))?(\S*)(?:\s.*)?$`; absent minor or patch is `0`; non-empty group 4 is a prerelease |
| `baseline` | **obligation K-8** | **obligation K-8** |
| `compatibility` | **empty until qualification** | **empty until qualification** |
| `platforms` | **empty** | **empty** |

The `jdk` normalization is **MEASURED**: `java --version` writes
`openjdk 26.0.1 2026-04-21` to stdout with an empty stderr and normalizes to
`(26, 0, 1)`, while `java -version` writes a differently-shaped, quoted banner
to **stderr** with an empty stdout. The single-stream form is the narrower
surface and is the one the entry pins. A legacy `1.8.0_452` banner normalizes to
`(1, 8, 0)` with a non-empty remainder and is a prerelease under this rule; it is
rejected either way once K-8 fixes a baseline above it, and the entry states that
rather than special-casing the legacy scheme.

`compatibility` is **empty**, not populated, and that is not an omission.
Decision 0007 section 1.1.1 admits a family only after it has been tested
against **that driver's conformance vectors**. No Kotlin vectors exist, so no
family has been tested, so no family may be listed. A populated set here would
be a fabricated claim of exactly the kind decision 0007 section 1.3 exists to
prevent. The same argument gives `platforms` the empty set, independently of the
platform position in section 9.

Because both sets are empty, the `kotlin` and `jdk` entries are **reserved and
incomplete** after this record. That is the correct state: decision 0007
requires them to be completed "on a qualified host", and no such host exists.
This record fixes their shape, their metadata source, their probe stream
discipline, and the decision rule for every remaining field, so that
qualification fills them mechanically rather than re-deciding them.

### 9. Platform position and the qualification protocol

**DECIDED.** `platforms` holds the **empty set** for both identifiers. No
platform is claimed, and macOS is not claimed merely because the design was
reasoned about on a macOS host. Decision 0008 section 9 anticipates exactly
this: every reserved identifier starts with an empty qualified-platform set.

A `(driver, operating_system, architecture)` tuple enters the set only when a
qualification run on that exact tuple, on an immutable native host, produces
**all** of the following, each with recorded argv and real exit code:

| # | Requirement |
|---|---|
| Q1 | The Kotlin/Native distribution resolves from a trusted origin, and obligations K-1 and K-7 are answered: the trusted launcher, the compiler entry point, the version probe with its named stream, and the anchored normalization rule. |
| Q2 | A shim-`PATH` run of the full compile records **0** `PATH` resolutions, **and** the paired control run through the shipped launcher records a non-zero count. Without the control firing, the zero proves nothing. |
| Q3 | Every child process the compiler spawns is a regular file inside `<kotlin_root>` or `<jdk_root>`, recorded by resolved absolute path. Any other executable fails the tuple (obligation K-2). |
| Q4 | With `KONAN_DATA_DIR` pointed at an operation-private empty directory and no reachable network, the compile succeeds and that directory is unchanged, **or** the tuple fails. A distribution that downloads is not qualified (obligation K-3). |
| Q5 | The compiler produces exactly one file for the build; its exact name is recorded (obligation K-4); it is renamed inside operation-private staging only; and no by-product is published. |
| Q6 | The published file is a native executable for the tuple, runs, and its dynamic dependencies are all base-installation libraries of that platform. |
| Q7 | The default native target is read from the distribution's own output and mapped to the claim vocabulary `(operating_system, architecture)` (obligation K-6). |
| Q8 | The allow-list of section 6 is exercised with at least one positive case and one rejected case per subsumed surface row, and the `@`-name case is included. |
| Q9 | `compatibility` gains the tested family, and only that family, after the driver's conformance vectors pass against it. |

Per-platform requirements on top of that common test:

- **macOS.** The Apple-platform linking path is the decisive question. If any
  Xcode-provided or `/usr/bin`-resolved executable — `clang`, `ld`, `libtool`,
  `xcrun`, `dsymutil`, `install_name_tool`, `codesign` — is spawned, the tuple
  fails Q3 and macOS is **not admitted**, because there is no toolchain
  identifier for a platform SDK that contributes a process. An implementation
  MUST NOT answer this by resolving a host tool, by shipping a shim, or by
  declaring the SDK data-only while executing from it.
- **Windows.** An implementation MUST NOT resolve `cmd.exe`, `powershell.exe`,
  `link.exe`, `cl.exe`, `lib.exe`, `vswhere.exe`, or a Visual Studio activation
  script. The MinGW-family linking path Kotlin/Native ships must be shown to
  live inside `<kotlin_root>`. Windows `arm64` is a separate tuple from
  `amd64` and neither is implied by the other. `TASK-260729-rhjxtx` measured
  that no Kotlin toolchain of any backend exists on the reachable Windows host.
- **Linux.** Excluded until `TASK-260728-1skseh`, then qualified by
  `TASK-260728-3u1nho` with the identical test plus the base-installation
  dependency check of Q6.

The obligations referenced above, collected:

| # | Question | Failure consequence |
|---|---|---|
| K-1 | Trusted launcher shape: JVM main class by `-cp` (a), or a self-contained regular executable (b) | neither ⇒ section 10 |
| K-2 | Every spawned child inside a fingerprinted root | that platform is excluded |
| K-3 | Distribution self-contained; no `~/.konan` fetch | that platform is excluded |
| K-4 | Exact produced filename and suffix | blocks Q5 |
| K-5 | `.kts` behaviour under the native backend | tightens section 6's diagnostic only; the allow-list already rejects it |
| K-6 | Default native target spelling and mapping | blocks Q7 |
| K-7 | `kotlin` `primary_relpath`, `probe` and `normalization`, with the stream named | blocks Q1 |
| K-8 | `kotlin` and `jdk` baselines, from the distribution's own stated requirement | blocks admission |

### 10. Retirement trigger

**DECIDED.** Decision 0008 section 10 requires this record to establish, not
negotiate, whether a Kotlin backend satisfies section 3. The artifact model
question is answered — Kotlin/Native does, and no JVM shape does. The remaining
risk is that no *platform* qualifies, and that is fixed here rather than left
open:

Both `kotlin-native-v1` and `kotlin-native-repository-v1` are **retired unused**
if, at the moment `TASK-260728-251p01` mints manifest schema 8, no
`(driver, operating_system, architecture)` tuple has passed section 9. Retired
means what decision 0008 section 2 says it means: the identifiers are not
reassigned to another language, backend, artifact class, or source mode, and are
not enabled by relaxing another driver. Claim schema 4 is then minted over the
admitted set without them — six identifiers rather than eight — and both become
structurally unassertable rather than merely unclaimed.

A tuple that fails Q1 through Q9 is excluded rather than shipped with a
compensating sidecar, a host-resolved tool, a generated launcher, a downgraded
control, or a second published file. Partial admission is per tuple and never
per driver: a driver admitted on one tuple is not thereby admitted on another.

### 11. Capability limitations, stated rather than discovered

**DECIDED.** These are consequences of the design, not defects, and an authoring
guide MUST state all four:

1. **No third-party dependencies.** A package cannot supply or name a `.klib`,
   and the driver passes no `-library`. A Kotlin build root compiles against the
   distribution's own standard library and nothing else. This is stricter than
   Go's vendored model and than Rust's vendored model, and it is what removes
   the dependency-resolution graph phase entirely.
2. **No C interop.** `cinterop` is a second tool and a second command;
   `manager-worker-v2`'s session admits neither, and `.def` files are rejected
   by section 6.
3. **The build root is not an IDE project.** Section 6's allow-list excludes
   Gradle files, so an author who wants IDE support keeps the project outside
   the build root and the build root holds only `.kt` sources plus
   `kotlin-native-module.json`. Build roots are context-excluded and never
   runtime-copied, so this costs the agent nothing and costs the author a
   duplicated source layout or a build step outside Curator.
4. **No cross-compilation.** One host builds for its own target only.

### 12. Downstream obligations

- `TASK-260728-1koh5v`, `TASK-260728-gmfxdg` (Curator) and
  `TASK-260728-3ar1qp`, `TASK-260728-1uj0bc` (csk): implement against
  `docs/kotlin-native-build-drivers.md`, and implement **no** platform as
  claimed until section 9 passes for its tuple.
- `TASK-260728-2bu2q6`: qualify the candidate; it owns Q1 through Q9 and the
  eight obligations, and it MUST NOT fill a registry field by assertion.
- `TASK-260728-3u1nho`: Linux qualification, after `TASK-260728-1skseh`.
- `TASK-260728-251p01`: integrate only if at least one tuple qualified; otherwise
  apply section 10 and mint claim schema 4 without the two identifiers.
- `TASK-260728-2uh7em`: authoring and operations guidance, which MUST carry
  section 11 in full and MUST NOT present a Gradle project as a supported
  layout.
- `TASK-260728-1g0z69`'s reserved `kotlin` and `jdk` entries are completed by
  this record only as far as section 8 states; the empty `compatibility` and
  `platforms` sets are the current normative values, not placeholders to be
