## Stable failure classes

No new architecture-level class is introduced. Kotlin's per-surface diagnostics
sit beneath decision 0008's existing classes:

- `build_package_code_execution_forbidden` — the section 6 allow-list, with
  per-surface diagnostics `kotlin_response_file_name_forbidden`,
  `kotlin_script_source_forbidden`, `kotlin_build_system_file_forbidden`,
  `kotlin_native_interop_input_forbidden`, `kotlin_prebuilt_library_forbidden`,
  `kotlin_non_source_entry_forbidden`, and `kotlin_non_regular_entry_forbidden`;
- `build_artifact_class_unsupported` — a platform that cannot produce a single
  directly loadable file;
- `build_toolchain_metadata_mismatch` — `kotlin-native-module.json` shape or
  `kotlin_version` comparison;
- `build_descriptor_driver_unsupported` and
  `build_descriptor_schema_unsupported` — unchanged from decision 0008 section 5.

## Rejected alternatives

- **A Kotlin/JVM driver with a fat JAR, arguing that one file satisfies section
  3.** Rejected: section 3 requires the file to be directly executable by the
  host program loader. A JAR is not, and making it so requires a
  manager-generated launcher — the exact `runtime-bundle` member section 3
  names. One file is necessary and not sufficient.
- **A Kotlin/JVM driver with a manager-generated launcher script.** Rejected:
  the launcher's contents would be derived from package data (main class,
  classpath), which is manager-authored executable content built from untrusted
  input — the install-time execution surface the protocol exists to exclude.
- **Kotlin/JVM plus GraalVM `native-image`.** Rejected on four independent
  grounds, any one sufficient: it needs a second compile-phase command, which
  `manager-worker-v2`'s session shape refuses; it needs a third toolchain
  outside decision 0007's closed identifier set; assembling its classpath needs
  either a package manager or a package-controlled classpath member, both
  refused by section 4; and it is a different backend, so decision 0008 section
  2 requires a different family segment and forbids reusing these identifiers.
- **Invoking `kotlinc-native`/`konanc` and neutralising the environment it
  reads.** Rejected on measurement: the launcher family resolves `bash`,
  `uname`, `dirname`, and `java` from `PATH`, so neutralising `JAVACMD` and
  friends still leaves four resolutions outside every fingerprinted tree.
  Bypassing the script closes all of them and was measured at 0 resolutions.
- **Gradle in any role, including a metadata-only query.** Rejected on
  measurement: `TASK-260729-rhjxtx` measured that `gradle properties` compiles
  the build script as a program source unit before answering. There is no
  read-only Gradle. This is the generic escape hatch decisions 0004, 0005, and
  0008 forbid, and no `--offline`, `--dry-run`, or init-script variant changes
  what it is.
- **Maven as the metadata file, reading only one field.** Rejected: the Maven
  project model is its plugin graph; reading one field while ignoring the graph
  would let a package ship a plugin declaration Curator silently does not run,
  which is worse than refusing the file.
- **`gradle.properties` as the metadata file because it is not executable.**
  Rejected: it is a Gradle input whose keys select compiler and daemon
  behaviour, so admitting it admits package-selected build behaviour under a
  name that merely looks inert.
- **A deny-list of forbidden filenames instead of the section 6 allow-list.**
  Rejected: Kotlin's code-execution surfaces are an open set, so a deny-list
  cannot be exhaustive, which decision 0008 section 7 requires. The `@` vector
  is the proof — it is a *name shape*, not a known filename.
- **Relying only on absolute-path argv to close the `@` vector, since that was
  measured sufficient.** Rejected as the sole layer: decision 0008 section 7
  requires the surface rejected deterministically *before* the compile phase,
  and an argv convention is one implementation slip from execution. It is kept
  as the structural backstop, with the measurement recorded.
- **Passing `source_dir` to the compiler as a directory source root.** Rejected:
  it hands file discovery to the compiler, so files the allow-list walk rejected
  could still be compiled, and decision 0008 section 4 forbids the manager
  inferring the source set.
- **Admitting cross-compilation via a package-selected or driver-selected
  target.** Rejected: a package-selected target is a forbidden command member,
  and a driver-selected one has no naming authority and reintroduces the
  representable-but-unserved-target gate for no capability the artifact model
  needs.
- **Naming a `compatibility` family or a `platforms` pair now, so the registry
  entry looks complete.** Rejected: decision 0007 section 1.1.1 admits a family
  only after testing against the driver's conformance vectors, which do not
  exist. An entry that looks complete and is not is worse than one that is
  visibly reserved.
- **Deferring the whole contract until a Kotlin/Native host exists.** Rejected:
  every host-independent question — artifact class, identifiers, metadata file,
  command surfaces, session shape, rejection matrix, environment, cache
  identity, capability limits — is decidable now, and deciding them now is what
  makes qualification mechanical instead of a second design pass. The
  host-dependent remainder is enumerated as eight named obligations rather than
  guessed.
- **Retiring both identifiers immediately, on the grounds that no host
  exists.** Rejected: absence of a host is not evidence that the backend fails
  section 3, and decision 0008 section 10 asks this record to *establish* the
  answer. Section 10 fixes a dated, checkable retirement trigger instead of
  either guessing.

## Compatibility impact

None on the wire. This record admits no identifier, mints no schema, and moves
no frozen byte. `go-v1` and `go-repository-v1` identities, `manager-worker-v1`,
`capability-evidence-v1`, `curator-go-toolchain-v1`, the rc.4 and rc.5
conformance corpora, and the rc.5 pin are untouched. Manifest schemas 1 through
7, descriptor schema 1, receipt schemas 1 and 2, marker schemas 1 through 3, and
claim schemas 1 through 3 continue to reject both Kotlin identifiers, as decision
0008 section 11 item 12 requires.

`kotlin-native-module.json` is a new **package-authored** file bound by a
reserved driver. It reaches no wire surface until manifest schema 8 is minted
with `kotlin-native-v1` admitted, and it is inert for every other driver.

Decision 0007's reserved `kotlin` and `jdk` entries move from "no fields" to
"shape, metadata source, probe discipline and decision rules fixed; probe,
relpath, baseline, `compatibility` and `platforms` outstanding". The `jdk`
companion is now REQUIRED under a **native** artifact model, which corrects the
"`jdk` if JVM" expectation in decision 0007's driver-mapping table; the reason is
that the Kotlin/Native compiler is JVM-hosted, not that the artifact needs a JVM.

## Security impact

Net positive, and concentrated in three places.

The measured `@`-response-file vector is a **new** finding about the Kotlin CLI:
a filename alone, with no build system and no manifest field, escalates to
arbitrary build-time code execution with a zero exit code. Any Kotlin build
integration that enumerates package files and passes them positionally has this
hole. This record closes it twice — by an allow-list that rejects the name
before the compile phase, and by an argv discipline measured to defeat the
expansion — and the second layer is a backstop rather than the rule.

Refusing the shipped launcher removes four `PATH` resolutions and six
environment-selected pipeline inputs (`JAVACMD`, `JAVA_HOME`, `JAVA_OPTS`,
`KOTLIN_RUNNER`, `KOTLIN_COMPILER`, `KOTLIN_TOOL`) from the process graph
structurally rather than by neutralisation, and `KOTLIN_COMPILER` in particular
selects the compiler main class — an environment-selected backend. The direct
form was measured at 0 resolutions.

The allow-list makes Kotlin's capability surface the smallest of the four
languages — no dependencies, no interop, no plugins, no scripts, no
cross-compilation — and section 11 states that as a limitation so it is not
later relaxed by someone who reads it as an oversight.

Two exposures are recorded rather than closed. Whether the Kotlin/Native
compiler spawns an executable outside the fingerprinted roots is obligation K-2
and is a platform-exclusion test, not a residual risk that ships. Whether the
distribution fetches dependencies is obligation K-3, with `KONAN_DATA_DIR` and a
no-network qualification run as the test. Neither is answered by assertion, and
a failure of either excludes the platform rather than being mitigated.

