   315	### 6. The pre-compile rejection matrix: an allow-list, not a deny-list
   316	
   317	**DECIDED.** Decision 0008 section 7 requires an exhaustive, deterministic,
   318	pre-compile rejection matrix over every package-selected code-execution surface.
   319	Kotlin's surfaces are unusually broad — a general-purpose script engine
   320	(Gradle), a script dialect the compiler itself can execute (`.kts`), annotation
   321	processing (kapt, KSP), compiler plugins, and C interop — so an enumerated
   322	deny-list would be an open set and could not be exhaustive.
   323	
   324	The matrix is therefore a **closed allow-list over the compiler-visible tree**,
   325	on the same principle decision 0008 section 11 item 6 applies to schema member
   326	sets. Computed from the validated immutable snapshot, before the compile phase,
   327	by a manager-side walk that runs no compiler:
   328	
   329	The build root and every directory below it admit exactly:
   330	
   331	1. directories, whose names match `^[A-Za-z0-9_][A-Za-z0-9_.-]*$`;
   332	2. regular files whose names match `^[A-Za-z0-9_][A-Za-z0-9_.-]*\.kt$`; and
   333	3. exactly one additional regular file, `kotlin-native-module.json`, directly in
   334	   the build root and nowhere else.
   335	
   336	Every other entry is rejected under `build_package_code_execution_forbidden`
   337	with a per-surface diagnostic. There is no exception, no opt-out, no advisory
   338	mode, and no configuration that widens it.
   339	
   340	This single rule subsumes, by construction rather than by enumeration:
   341	
   342	| Surface | How it is excluded |
   343	|---|---|
   344	| Gradle build scripts, settings, wrapper, `gradle.properties`, `.gradle/` | not `.kt`, not the metadata file |
   345	| Maven `pom.xml`, `.mvn/`, `mvnw` | same |
   346	| Kotlin script sources `.kts` | not `.kt` |
   347	| kapt/KSP configuration, `META-INF/services`, ProGuard rules | same |
   348	| C-interop `.def`, headers, `.c`/`.m`/`.cpp`, objects, archives, shared libraries | same |
   349	| prebuilt `.klib`, `.jar`, `.aar` | same |
   350	| symlinks, devices, sockets, FIFOs, hard-linked escapes | not regular files or directories |
   351	| dotfiles of every kind | leading character class |
   352	| **any name beginning with `@`** | leading character class |
   353	
   354	The `@` row is the one that is not obvious, and it is the reason the leading
   355	character class is load-bearing rather than cosmetic.
   356	
   357	**MEASURED — a build-root filename is an argv-injection channel.** The Kotlin
   358	CLI expands a bare `@`-prefixed argument as a response file. With a file named
   359	`@inject` containing `-script`, and a `.kts` file in the same directory:
   360	
   361	| Argument form | Expanded | Outcome |
   362	|---|---|---|
   363	| `@inject` (bare relative) | yes | `-script` injected; **package code executed**; marker file written; exit **0** |
   364	| `/abs/path/@inject` | no | `error: source entry is not a Kotlin file`, exit 1 |
   365	| `./@inject` | no | same error, exit 1 |
   366	| `@nonexistent` | n/a | `warning: argfile not found`, exit **0** — a missing response file is not an error |
   367	
   368	So a package that ships a file named `@x` in a directory the driver enumerates
   369	can execute arbitrary Kotlin at build time, through the compiler alone, with no
   370	Gradle anywhere and with a zero exit code. Two independent layers close it, and
   371	the contract requires both:
   372	
   373	- **the normative layer** is the allow-list above, which rejects the name before
   374	  the compile phase, as decision 0008 section 7 demands; and
   375	- **the structural backstop** is section 7's argv discipline — every
   376	  package-derived path is passed in absolute form — which the table above
   377	  measures as sufficient on its own. It is a backstop and not the rule, because
   378	  relying on argv shaping alone would put the whole surface one implementation
   379	  slip away from execution.
   380	
   381	A `.kts` file that reaches the compiler *without* `-script` was measured to be
   382	compiled into a class and **not** executed (no marker; a `.class` produced, exit
   383	0) on the JVM backend. That is why `.kts` is rejected as a build-model surface
   384	rather than described as immediate execution: the honest statement is that it is
   385	inert alone and is the payload half of the `@` vector. Its behaviour under the
   386	native backend is UNVERIFIED and is obligation K-5.
   387	
   388	The four argv-only plugin surfaces — `-Xplugin=<path>`, `-Xcompiler-plugin=…`,
   389	`-P plugin:…`, and `-script-templates` — are **structurally unreachable**,
   390	because the closed command surface of section 4 gives a package no way to supply
   391	an argument and the driver's vector is fixed. They are named here so that the
   392	matrix is exhaustive over surfaces rather than over file names, and so that any
   393	future change that lets package data reach argv is visibly a change to this row.
