   102	### 2. Two disjoint closed sets: admitted wire, reserved namespace
   103	
   104	The driver identifier space is closed by enumeration, never by grammar,
   105	detection, or a family prefix. It is partitioned into exactly two disjoint sets
   106	with different normative force, and every implementer, schema, validator, and
   107	claim reader MUST keep them apart.
   108	
   109	**Admitted wire driver set.** Exactly two values, and this is the only set any
   110	schema, validator, manager, or claim reader may accept today:
   111	
   112	| Driver | Language family | Source mode | Receipt schema | Execution policy | Admitted |
   113	|---|---|---|---|---|---|
   114	| `go-v1` | Go | local snapshot | 1 | `manager-worker-v1` | rc.4 |
   115	| `go-repository-v1` | Go | external repository | 2 | `manager-worker-v1` | rc.5 |
   116	
   117	**Reserved driver namespace.** Exactly six values, closed by this decision and
   118	admitted by none of manifest schemas 1 through 7, `skill-build.json` schema 1,
   119	receipt schemas 1 and 2, marker schemas 1 through 3, or claim schemas 1 through
   120	3. They are names held against future admission, not wire values:
   121	
   122	| Driver | Language family | Source mode | Receipt schema | Execution policy | Status |
   123	|---|---|---|---|---|---|
   124	| `rust-v1` | Rust | local snapshot | 3 | `manager-worker-v2` | reserved |
   125	| `rust-repository-v1` | Rust | external repository | 4 | `manager-worker-v2` | reserved |
   126	| `swift-v1` | Swift | local snapshot | 3 | `manager-worker-v2` | reserved |
   127	| `swift-repository-v1` | Swift | external repository | 4 | `manager-worker-v2` | reserved |
   128	| `kotlin-native-v1` | Kotlin | local snapshot | 3 | `manager-worker-v2` | reserved |
   129	| `kotlin-native-repository-v1` | Kotlin | external repository | 4 | `manager-worker-v2` | reserved |
   130	
   131	The union of the two sets is the complete Protocol 1.0 build-driver identifier
   132	space. No other identifier may be coined at Protocol 1.0, in either set, by any
   133	task, schema, profile, or implementation.
   134	
   135	The identifier form is `<family>-v<n>` for the local source mode and
   136	`<family>-repository-v<n>` for the external source mode, matching the deployed
   137	Go pair. The Kotlin family segment carries the `native` backend qualifier
   138	because Kotlin has two candidate backends and only the native one can satisfy
   139	section 3; a later JVM-oriented driver, if it is ever reviewed and accepted,
   140	MUST use a different family segment and MUST NOT reuse these two identifiers.
   141	
   142	Reservation is not admission, and the two sets never overlap. A reserved
   143	identifier leaves the reserved namespace and enters the admitted wire driver set
   144	in exactly one way: its own driver contract is accepted, and
   145	`TASK-260728-251p01` moves it in the same change that mints the schema version
   146	admitting it. There is no partial state — until that move, every schema
   147	including manifest schema 8 as first minted MUST reject it, and a manager MUST
   148	treat it as an unknown driver. A reserved identifier whose contract is rejected
   149	is retired unused: it MUST NOT be reassigned to a different language, backend,
   150	artifact class, or source mode, and it MUST NOT be enabled by relaxing another
   151	driver.
   152	
   153	The receipt-schema and execution-policy columns of the reserved table are the
   154	allocation each identifier will carry when it is admitted. They are binding on
   155	the integration task and they are not wire facts today.
   156	
   157	Every schema that expresses a driver MUST express it as a `const` inside a
   158	`oneOf` over the admitted identifiers. A driver field MUST NOT be an open
   159	`enum`, a pattern, a bare string, or a value derived from a file extension,
   160	project-metadata filename, directory layout, or any other language detection. No
   161	manifest, descriptor, repository, substitution, or receipt may contain a
   162	`language`, `toolchain_family`, `build_system`, `backend`, or comparable
   163	selector.
   164	
   165	### 3. Artifact class: `native-executable-v1` only
   166	
   167	This version admits exactly one artifact class, `native-executable-v1`:
   168	
   169	- exactly one bounded regular file, produced into operation-private manager
   170	  staging, hashed there, and published immutably under the manager-home
   171	  mutation lock;
   172	- named solely by the manager from the consuming manifest command key, as
   173	  `bin/<command>` on Unix and `bin/<command>.exe` on Windows, exactly as
   174	  `protocol/core.md` sections 4.2 and 4.2.2 already require;
   175	- directly executable by the host program loader using only libraries the target
   176	  platform provides in its base installation, as fixed by the driver's own
   177	  platform matrix; and
   178	- never executed by the manager during validation, installation, status, repair,
   179	  rollback, or garbage collection.
   180	
   181	On the wire, `native-executable-v1` is carried by the shared `buildArtifactV1`
   182	definition, and that definition MUST be an object schema — `"type": "object"`
   183	exactly, never omitted, never unioned with a scalar, and never expressed as a
   184	boolean schema — requiring exactly `path`, `sha256`, and `size`, closed with
   185	`additionalProperties: false`, with each of the three bound to the canonical
   186	shared `portablePath`, `sha256`, and `nonNegativeSafeInteger` definitions. Each
   187	of those clauses is load-bearing rather than stylistic, for the same reason the
   188	toolchain requirement object needs its type: `properties`, `required`, and
   189	`additionalProperties` constrain objects only. A definition that keeps the three
   190	member names while declaring itself a string, an `object`/`string` union, or no
   191	type at all admits a bare launcher name where a published file belongs, and a
   192	definition that empties `required` admits an artifact asserting nothing — in both
   193	cases every existing positive case still validates, so the widening is invisible
   194	to a member-name comparison. Both readmit the rejected `runtime-bundle` class
   195	through the definition that is supposed to exclude it, which is why the gate in
   196	section 11 holds this definition to an exact object schema and additionally
   197	proves against the compiled receipt validators that a scalar, an array, an empty
   198	object, a missing member, an extra runtime member, a non-portable path, an
   199	unprefixed digest, and a negative size are all rejected.
   200	
   201	Binding the three members to shared definitions is a closure only while those
   202	definitions still mean what this section fixes, so `portablePath`, `sha256`, and
   203	`nonNegativeSafeInteger` MUST each remain exactly the schema the frozen rc.5
   204	corpus ships. The path grammar states where a published file may live, the digest
   205	alphabet is the whole description of what it contains, and the size ceiling is
   206	the range in which the manager's recorded size is meaningful; none of the three
   207	is an illustrative example. A single keyword is enough to move any of them —
   208	`maxLength` raised by one, uppercase added to the digest alphabet, the
   209	safe-integer ceiling lifted or dropped — and such a change leaves the pinned
   210	`$ref` intact, leaves every published positive case valid, and leaves every
   211	individual bad value a gate might sample still rejected, while the compiled
   212	receipt validator begins accepting an artifact this section does not admit.
   213	Enforcement therefore MUST be structural on those three definitions, and a proof
   214	by sampled rejections MUST NOT be presented as covering them.
   215	
   216	No driver may publish a second file. Debug information, separate symbol files,
   217	program databases, import libraries, module or interface files, resource
   218	bundles, shared libraries, sysroot copies, and incremental-build state are
   219	compiler by-products. They remain in operation-private staging, are discarded
   220	with it, and MUST NOT enter cache identity, the receipt, the marker, the shim
   221	relationship, or publication.
   222	
   223	The `runtime-bundle` class is rejected for this version. A runtime bundle is any
   224	artifact that requires a manager-generated launcher, an interpreter or virtual
   225	machine, a classpath or module path, a runtime image, or more than one published
   226	file. It is rejected because:
   227	
   228	- receipts, markers, shims, currentness, and garbage collection are all built on
   229	  exactly one artifact path and one artifact digest, and a bundle would require
   230	  every one of those identities to be redefined;
   231	- a manager-generated launcher is manager-authored executable content whose
   232	  contents would be derived from package data such as a main class and a
   233	  classpath, which reintroduces the install-time execution surface the protocol
   234	  exists to exclude;
   235	- the required runtime would be an execution-time dependency the manager cannot
   236	  fingerprint, cannot bind into cache identity, and cannot verify at install
   237	  time, so a marker could claim currentness for an artifact that no longer runs;
   238	  and
   239	- `protocol/core.md` section 12.1 requires a shim to point exactly at the
   240	  marker-selected protected artifact, which a bundle cannot satisfy without
   241	  widening that rule.
   242	
   243	A driver and platform pair that can only produce an artifact needing a
   244	manager-published or manager-installed sidecar runtime file MUST NOT be admitted
   245	for that platform in this version. It fails with
   246	`build_artifact_class_unsupported` rather than gaining a sidecar, a launcher, or
   247	an installer step. A future runtime-bundle profile requires its own artifact
   248	class identity, its own receipt, marker, and claim schema versions, a launcher
   249	generation contract, a runtime identity and verification contract, and its own
   250	review. It MUST NOT be admitted by widening `native-executable-v1` or any of the
   251	eight driver identifiers.
   252	
   253	This version does not require bit-reproducible artifacts. Cache identity is
   254	keyed on the canonical build input, and the artifact digest records what a
   255	specific operation produced. A toolchain step that legitimately embeds
   256	non-reproducible bytes, such as a linker-applied ad-hoc signature, is compiler
   257	output and not a manager signing step, but it MUST be produced by the driver's
   258	fixed argument vector without selecting a signing identity, credential, or
   259	network interaction.
   260	
